  "generated_text": "First, we need to subtract \\(\\frac{1}{8}\\) from \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\). To do this, we need a common denominator. The least common multiple of 4 and 8 is 8.\n\nNext, we convert \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) to a fraction with a denominator of 8. We do this by multiplying the numerator and the denominator of \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) by 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3 \\times 2}{4 \\times 2} = \\frac{6}{8}\n\\]\n\nNow, we subtract \\(\\frac{1}{8}\\) from \\(\\frac{6}{8}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{8} - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{6 - 1}{8} = \\frac{5}{8}\n\\]\n\nSo, the subtraction of \\(\\frac{1}{8}\\) from \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{8}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{8}}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[5/8, '\\\\frac{5}{8}']",
    "gold_answer": "We are looking for $\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{1}{8}$. The least common denominator is 8, so we must rewrite $\\frac{3}{4}$ with 8 in the denominator.   $\\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{2} = \\frac{6}{8}$.   Then, we know that $\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{6}{8} - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{6-1}{8} = \\boxed{\\frac{5}{8}}$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
